From 7dbc5469c50cc9427bae67d85527ce263c572d6b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 20:08:27 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] security: the audit's Medium and Low remainder, and two
 defects it uncovered

Closes the 2026-08-26 audit: the 17 Medium and 14 Low findings, plus two
defects that only surfaced while validating the fixes on hardware. The two
late ones are the reason this rung exists rather than shipping v2.8.0.

WARDEN LISTS WERE BEING DESTROYED BY THEIR OWN MIGRATION (the serious one).
  rc's nvram_safe_get() returns EMPTY for these keys above roughly 200-400
  bytes while `nvram get` from a shell returns the same key in full - 602
  bytes captured verbatim in the same shell where the library call had
  yielded nothing. Measured: 186 bytes read fine, 381 and 706 both came back
  empty. The one-shot nvram->/jffs migration therefore read "", wrote a
  0-byte file, and then unset nvram: the list was not truncated, it was GONE.
  The /jffs store was never at fault - a 706-byte list written straight into
  it produces a fully populated set.
  Fixed two ways, because either alone is a single point of failure:
  rw_nvram_cli_get() reads through the path that demonstrably works, and the
  nvram originals are now DELIBERATELY LEFT IN PLACE so the worst case is
  "the list did not migrate and is still where it was" instead of "the list
  is gone". WHY the two read paths disagree is NOT established and is on the
  backlog; this rung removes the data loss, not the mystery. The limit is not
  ours and predates the store: a Warden ban list over ~256 bytes never
  enforced in nvram either.
  Proven on metal: a 912-byte, 60-entry list migrates byte-for-byte
  identical, the ipset populates to 60, and nvram still holds the original.

THE LAN ANTI-LOCKOUT RULE WAS BUILT FROM AN EMPTY VARIABLE.
  `%%` in an fprintf format string yields ONE `%`, so the generated shell
  carried ${LANIP%.*} where ${LANIP%%.*} was intended. LANNET came out empty
  and the structural anti-lockout RETURN - the rule that keeps a LAN client
  from being dropped by a feed - was malformed. Found only by reading the
  GENERATED script on the router; a green build, a clean cppcheck and a
  passing staged-symbol grep were all blind to it. Logic now proven across
  /24, /25 (both halves), /22, /16, /8 and bogus input.

AND THE FALSE ALARM THE MIGRATION FIX UNCOVERED. With the manual ban list
  finally usable, rw_gen_apply()'s generated script ends on the
  rw_emit_ban_conntrack() block, whose last statement is `conntrack -D`. That
  returns 1 when no live flow matches - the normal case for a freshly-banned
  address - and a script's exit status is its last command's. start_rwarden()
  treats any non-zero exit as the cold-boot LAN deferral, so it logged "LAN not
  ready at boot - protection deferred" on every start with a non-empty ban list
  while protection was fully armed, and made the GENUINE deferral
  indistinguishable from that noise. The script now ends with an explicit
  `exit 0`. Measured on metal: exits 1 with a 60-entry list present, 0 with only
  those lines stripped. Invisible before this rung because an empty ban list
  emits no conntrack lines at all.

MEDIUM (17) - atomic publish for the Warden update/fold/stats writes; a
  geo-fetch deadline so one slow custom feed cannot eat the window; ipset
  destroy-on-type-mismatch; the DNAT target restricted to a single IPv4 host;
  a CSRF gate on reaper_conn.cgi; an LRU eviction in gkd; a conntrack probe
  bound by a shell-side timeout; rjf_get() gained a three-state exists so a
  file that opens but cannot be read is no longer indistinguishable from an
  absent one; rjf_syncdir() fsyncs the parent after rename; the rules engine
  loads its confirmed config when the engine is on; the engine-off branch
  promotes only obj/grp; gk_refresh became a real service verb; the armed
  check consults pids("gkd"); a proto-31 netlink socket-count detector in
  rwatch (log-only, never auto-kill); rexport's nine bare `nvram get` calls
  moved behind the _nv guard.

LOW (14) - LANNET computed arithmetically rather than by string surgery; /0
  rejected; a per-family blog skip count; PBR rules deleted by their own
  fwmark; the six reaper_fw_list_get calls hoisted out of a loop; the scrape
  token fails closed; reload_upnp consults upnp_enabled(); a weak http_id is
  logged; gkd refuses a pre-NTP time grant; Gatekeeper logs list truncation
  per dropped MAC.

Also: the _nv temp path is created under `set -C` (O_CREAT|O_EXCL) after an
unlink, so a pre-planted symlink can no longer turn a guarded nvram read into
a root truncate-and-write.

Verified on live RT-BE96U hardware, not just at compile time: C1 exercised in
both directions (engine off -> chains absent, no orphan apply.sh.tmp, teardown
script consumed; engine on -> chains restored to the same rule counts and the
same apply.sh digest); the Warden migration test above; zero bare `nvram get`
outside the _nv helper across all five generated scripts; every Reaper store
and tmpfs directory at 0700; rtrafd flat at 3.12s CPU.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
